                },
                {
                  "id": "h-side-by-side",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Aluminum vs Stainless Steel Dry Van Trailer: Side-by-Side",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "side-by-side"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-side-by-side-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Here's a straightforward comparison to help you evaluate your options:",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "compare-side-by-side-2",
                  "type": "compare-table",
                  "props": {
                    "headA": "Feature",
                    "headB": "Stainless Steel",
                    "headC": "Aluminum",
                    "rows": [
                      {
                        "a": "Durability",
                        "b": "High, impact-resistant",
                        "c": "Moderate"
                      },
                      {
                        "a": "Weight",
                        "b": "Heavier",
                        "c": "Lightweight"
                      },
                      {
                        "a": "Fuel Efficiency",
                        "b": "Lower",
                        "c": "Higher"
                      },
                      {
                        "a": "Maintenance",
                        "b": "Lower long-term",
                        "c": "Potentially higher over time"
                      },
                      {
                        "a": "Lifespan",
                        "b": "Long-lasting",
                        "c": "Moderate to long"
                      },
                      {
                        "a": "Best For",
                        "b": "Heavy-duty, long-term use",
                        "c": "Fuel savings, lighter loads"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-side-by-side-3",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The right choice depends on how you operate, not just what looks good on paper.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-side-by-side-4",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "When a Stainless Steel Dry Van Trailer Makes Sense",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-side-by-side-5",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "A stainless steel dry van trailer for sale is often the better choice if you're running long-haul routes, your loads involve heavier or rougher freight, you operate in high-humidity or coastal environments, or you want fewer long-term repairs. This option is built for consistency, and for many operators, consistency is what keeps customers happy and revenue steady.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-side-by-side-6",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "When Aluminum Is the Better Fit",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-side-by-side-7",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "An aluminum dry van trailer for sale may be the right move if fuel efficiency is a top priority, you're running lighter freight, you want to maximize payload capacity, or you're scaling your fleet quickly. If your margins depend on efficiency per mile, aluminum can give you an edge.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "fig-2",
                  "type": "image",
                  "props": {
                    "width": "full",
                    "image": {
                      "src": "/img/blog/aluminum-vs-stainless-steel-dry-van-trailer/2.jpg",
                      "width": 1000,
                      "height": 667,
                      "alt": "Sun State technician checking a dry van trailer's rear locking hardware"
                    }
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-cost-roi",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Cost vs ROI: What Really Matters",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "cost-roi"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-cost-roi-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "It's easy to focus on upfront cost, but the smarter question is: what will this trailer cost you over time? A stainless steel dry van for sale may come with a higher initial investment, but it may require fewer repairs, it can last longer under heavy use, and it reduces downtime. On the other hand, aluminum may save money upfront and improve fuel efficiency, but could require more attention depending on how it's used.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "fig-3",
                  "type": "image",
                  "props": {
                    "width": "full",
                    "image": {
                      "src": "/img/blog/aluminum-vs-stainless-steel-dry-van-trailer/3.jpg",
                      "width": 1000,
                      "height": 667,
                      "alt": "Close-up of a dry van trailer's rear lighting and structural detail"
                    }
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-choosing",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Choosing the Right Stainless Steel Dry Van Trailer",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "choosing"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-choosing-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "When comparing an aluminum vs stainless steel dry van trailer, there's no one-size-fits-all answer. If your priority is durability, longevity, and reduced downtime, a stainless steel dry van trailer is a strong investment. If your focus is fuel efficiency and maximizing payload, aluminum may be the better option.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-choosing-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The right choice comes down to how you run your business and what you need your equipment to deliver every day. Ready to find the right trailer for your operation? <a href=\"/locations/trailer-sales\">Sun State Trailers</a> is here to help you choose equipment you can count on, mile after mile.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
